<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Gobind Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1666">1666</a>–<a href="/years/1708">1708</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guru Gobind Singh was the tenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> Guru, who in 1699 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>, the order of initiated Sikhs. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> hill chiefs of the Sivaliks, including Bilaspur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> at Bhangani in 1688, and Mughal forces. His two youngest sons were executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a> in 1704, an act the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a> protested.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh.jpg" alt="Guru Gobind Singh" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Guru_Gobind_Singh,_found_within_the_Anandpuri_Hazuri_Bir_codex_of_the_Dasam_Granth,_ca.1690%27s%E2%80%931700%27s_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guru Gobind Singh was the only son of Guru Tegh Bahadur, the ninth Sikh Guru, and Mata Gujri, and was born into the Sodhi clan of the Punjabi Khatri community. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, while his father was visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, and his birth name was Gobind Das. His family returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1670 and in March 1672 moved to Chakk Nanaki in the Sivalik range, where he was schooled. In 1675, at the age of nine, he was formally made leader of the Sikhs after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> had his father executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1699 the Guru asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> to gather at Anandpur on Vaisakhi. According to Sikh tradition he asked for a volunteer and took him into a tent, returning with a bloody sword, and repeated this four more times. After the fifth volunteer, he came back with all five unharmed and called them the Panj Pyare, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>. He is credited with introducing the Five Ks, the articles of faith that Khalsa Sikhs wear at all times.</p><p>The Guru is credited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition with finalizing the Kartarpur manuscript of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, including the compositions of his father, and with declaring it the eternal Guru. He is also credited with the Dasam Granth, which some Sikhs treat as a second scripture and others consider its authority disputed.</p><p>He fought 21 battles against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and the kings of the Siwalik Hills, among them Bhangani in 1688 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadaun">Nadaun</a> in 1691. He held that war was a last resort, a Dharamyudh fought in defence of righteousness. Mughal court historians wrote in a hostile tone, holding that he had corrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition by creating a military order willing to resist the imperial army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His four sons died in his lifetime. The elder two, Ajit Singh, aged 17, and Jujhar Singh, aged 13, were killed in the Battle of Chamkaur in December 1704, and the younger two were executed by the Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a>. He then wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zafarnama">Zafarnama</a>, a defiant Persian letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, stern yet conciliatory, which accuses the emperor of immorality in governance and in war and predicts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> would soon end.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> died in 1707 the Guru supported Bahadur Shah I in the Battle of Jajau, and then stayed near the imperial camp for nearly a year hoping to recover Anandpur, but Bahadur Shah kept postponing any restoration. Guru Gobind Singh died on 7 October 1708. He is remembered as the tenth and last human Sikh Guru, and he established the concept of Guru <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panth">Panth</a> as his spiritual successor, though it is rarely invoked today.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guru Gobind Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Guru Gobind Singh</b> added the hymns of his father, Guru Tegh Bahadur, and before his death in 1708 declared that the Granth would be the Guru after him.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>A related text, the Jaap Sahib of <b>Guru Gobind Singh</b>, opens the Dasam Granth; it is longer, at 199 stanzas, chiefly in Braj-Hindi and Sanskrit, and lists some 950 names of God.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li><li><blockquote>Raja Bhim Chand fought <b>Guru Gobind Singh</b> in the 1680s and 1690s, and the state was later overrun by the Gurkhas of the Kingdom of Nepal.</blockquote><a href="/state/bilaspur-state">Bilaspur State</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Rajas, who used the title Shah from the 16th century, fought with Kumaon and the Mughals, and Fateh Shah opposed <b>Guru Gobind Singh</b> at Bhangani in 1688.</blockquote><a href="/state/garhwal-kingdom">Garhwal Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1705 Nawab Sher Muhammad Khan, then serving the Mughal governor of Sirhind, protested against the execution of the young sons of <b>Guru Gobind Singh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/malerkotla-state">Malerkotla State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Nanded, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.61,85.14&amp;year=1693" aria-label="Patna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6803 1222 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guru Gobind Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7253 1492h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.61,85.14&amp;year=1693">What was here in 1693? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
